Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can use the `functions.B` helper to spin up a task queue with a concurrency of **5**.  
The queue will fire two events – `'saturated'` and `'unsaturated'` – which we hook into so that the console prints the messages you requested.

```js
/* 1️⃣  Create the queue ------------------------------------------- */
const queue = functions.B({
  /*  •  t   – concurrency level (stringified number) */
  t: "5",

  /*  •  e   – optional initial task(s).  We’ll add them later.     */
  /*  •  n   – optional payload size.  Omit it if you don’t need it. */
});

/* 2️⃣  Wire up the saturation events ------------------------------ */
queue.on('saturated', () => {
  console.log('Queue is saturated');
});

queue.on('unsaturated', () => {
  console.log('Queue is unsaturated');
});

/* 3️⃣  Add some demo tasks ---------------------------------------- */
for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  /*  Each task is a simple function that logs its own id.          */
  queue.add(() => {
    console.log(`Task ${i}`);
  });
}

/* 4️⃣  (Optional) Pause / resume / inspect the queue if needed ---- */
// queue.pause();
// queue.resume();
// console.log(queue.size());   // number of queued tasks
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| 1️⃣  | `functions.B` creates a new queue instance with a concurrency of 5. | The queue will run at most 5 tasks in parallel. |
| 2️⃣  | Event listeners are attached. | When the queue’s internal worker pool reaches the concurrency limit, the `'saturated'` event fires; when it drops below that limit, `'unsaturated'` fires. |
| 3️⃣  | Ten tasks are enqueued. | Each task simply logs its own identifier. Because the concurrency is 5, you’ll see five “Task X” messages appear almost simultaneously, followed by the next five once the first batch finishes. |
| 4️⃣  | (Optional) You can pause, resume, or inspect the queue. | Gives you full control over the queue’s lifecycle. |

> **Tip:** If you prefer to seed the queue with tasks right away, you can pass an array of function strings to the `e` field, e.g.  
> ```js
> const queue = functions.B({
>   t: "5",
>   e: "[() => console.log('Task 1'), () => console.log('Task 2')]"
> });
> ```

That’s all you need to get a fully‑functional, saturated‑aware task queue running!

